description:
Add a new /mermaid skill (skills/mermaid/SKILL.md, same shape as skills/create-task/SKILL.md: a fenced `!` block that runs a script) that invokes a new parser script scripts/mermaid/mermaid.ts. The script uses the already-installed TypeScript compiler (`ts.createSourceFile`, no new dependency) to turn each source file into a tree, walks that tree into a small in-between list of boxes {id, label} and edges {from, to}, then prints that list as mermaid text. The grammar is the one used by ~/Programming/relationship-mermaid (see diagrams/say-something-or-let-it-go.mmd there and the `Q_`/`Q_CHOICE` checks in graph-slice.ts), NOT the taskTools diagrams/tackle-tasks grammar. Files: `git ls-files '*.ts'` minus `*.test.ts` and `tests/`. Output: .taskTools/diagrams/<relative path>.mmd. Walk rules: each function-like declaration (function, arrow const, class method) starts its own chain at a top box; the file's top-level statements start a chain at a box named after the file; every statement is a B_ box with the source text as label; if/switch/for/while are Q_ diamonds with one Q_CHOICE_ arm each; if/loop arms are labelled with the condition text and its table-flipped opposite; loop bodies point back to their diamond; if arms rejoin after the if; an arm ending in return stops. Id rule and suffix rule are in the goal. Fallback: a statement the parser cannot shape into a diamond becomes a plain B_ box with the line text.

## Example sources for tests/mermaid.test.ts

1. Calls only:
```ts
function a() {}
function b() {}
function c() {}
function multipleCalls() { a(); b(); c(); a(); }
function singleCall() { a(); b(); }
```
expected:
```
flowchart TD
B_a1["a()"]
B_a2["a()"]
B_a3["a()"]
B_b1["b()"]
B_b2["b()"]
B_c["c()"]
B_multipleCalls["multipleCalls()"]
B_singleCall["singleCall()"]

B_multipleCalls --> B_a1 --> B_b1 --> B_c --> B_a2
B_singleCall --> B_a3 --> B_b2
```
(a, b, c have empty bodies and so no top box of their own.)

2. if with early return:
```ts
function load(path: string) {
  if (!exists(path)) {
    return null;
  }
  const raw = read(path);
  return parse(raw);
}
```
expected:
```
flowchart TD
B_load["load(path: string)"]
Q_not_exists_path{"if( !exists(path) )"}
Q_CHOICE_not_exists_path_Y["!exists(path)"]
Q_CHOICE_not_exists_path_N["exists(path)"]
B_read_path["const raw = read(path)"]
B_return_null["return null"]
B_return_parse_raw["return parse(raw)"]

B_load --> Q_not_exists_path
Q_not_exists_path --> Q_CHOICE_not_exists_path_Y
Q_CHOICE_not_exists_path_Y --> B_return_null
Q_not_exists_path --> Q_CHOICE_not_exists_path_N
Q_CHOICE_not_exists_path_N --> B_read_path
B_read_path --> B_return_parse_raw
```

3. for and while loops:
```ts
function sum(list: number[]) {
  let total = 0;
  for (const x of list) {
    total += x;
  }
  while (total > 100) {
    total = halve(total);
  }
  return total;
}
```
expected: Q_for_x_of_list{"for (const x of list)"} with Q_CHOICE_for_x_of_list_Y["i < list.length; x = list[i];"] and Q_CHOICE_for_x_of_list_N["i >= list.length"]; Q_while_total_greater_than_100{"while (total > 100)"} with _Y["total > 100"] and _N["total <= 100"]; B_total_plus_is_x --> Q_for_x_of_list and B_total_is_halve_total --> Q_while_total_greater_than_100 as back edges; the _N arms continue to the next statement; B_return_total last.

4. A pure script file with top-level statements and no functions: chain starts at B_<fileName>["<fileName>.ts"].

5. A switch with two cases and default: one diamond, three Q_CHOICE_ arms labelled with the case values and `default`.

## Follow-up

More example sources are needed later (class methods, nested if inside a loop, unparseable chained expressions like array.map().filter()). Record them as a follow-up task once the parser exists.
